  "request": {
    "spec": "Add a structured address form: the request may provide 'addressLine1', an optional 'addressLine2', 'city' and 'postcode'. Prefer the structured fields when present; the flat 'address' input remains accepted for backward compatibility. An owner is valid when it supplies an address in EITHER form (a non-blank 'addressLine1', or the flat 'address'), plus city; the request contract stays backward-compatible so earlier minimal owners are still accepted. This changes existing behaviour: normalization applies to whichever address fields are supplied, and everything that reads the address (the household hash, postcode validation and locality) uses the structured fields when present, falling back to the flat address. Also return a composed 'address' string equal to the normalized addressLine1, with a single space and the normalized addressLine2 appended when addressLine2 is present.",
    "prompt": "Implement the following change to the application so that it fully satisfies\nthe specification and all existing and new tests pass. Run the test suite and\nmake it green before finishing.\n\nAdd a structured address form: the request may provide 'addressLine1', an optional 'addressLine2', 'city' and 'postcode'. Prefer the structured fields when present; the flat 'address' input remains accepted for backward compatibility. An owner is valid when it supplies an address in EITHER form (a non-blank 'addressLine1', or the flat 'address'), plus city; the request contract stays backward-compatible so earlier minimal owners are still accepted. This changes existing behaviour: normalization applies to whichever address fields are supplied, and everything that reads the address (the household hash, postcode validation and locality) uses the structured fields when present, falling back to the flat address. Also return a composed 'address' string equal to the normalized addressLine1, with a single space and the normalized addressLine2 appended when addressLine2 is present.\n"
  },
